 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\n# OAuth2 response_type=none not working correctly\n\n## Description\n\nWhen using `response_type=none` in an authorization request, the state parameter is not being properly included in the redirect URL.\n\n## Steps to reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom oauthlib.oauth2 import WebApplicationServer\nfrom oauthlib.oauth2.rfc6749.endpoints.authorization import AuthorizationEndpoint\n\n# Create a server\nserver = WebApplicationServer(client_getter)\nendpoint = AuthorizationEndpoint(server)\n\n# Make a request with response_type=none and a state parameter\nuri = 'http://example.com/auth?response_type=none&client_id=client123&state=xyz'\nuri += '&redirect_uri=http%3A%2F%2Fclient.example.com%2Fcb'\n\n# Process the request\nheaders, body, status_code = endpoint.create_authorization_response(uri, scopes=['read'])\n\n# The Location header doesn't include the state parameter\nprint(headers['Location'])  # Outputs: http://client.example.com/cb\n# Expected: http://client.example.com/cb?state=xyz\n```\n\n## Actual behavior\n\nWhen using `response_type=none`, the state parameter is not included in the redirect URL. The server redirects to the redirect_uri without any parameters.\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nThe state parameter should be included in the redirect URL when `response_type=none` is used, as per the OAuth 2.0 specification which states that the state parameter should always be returned if it was present in the request.\n</issue>\n",
